    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d107">

                <pb facs="0230" n="116" sameAs="frus:frus1922v02/pg116"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">884.113/13</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Aden</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Davis</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Aden</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1922-11-07"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">November 7, 1922</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received
                        December 1.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 37</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the
                    Department’s Instruction dated June 10, 1922, File No. 884.113/6,<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d107-f1"/> instructing this Consulate to transmit any additional
                    information regarding the shipment of American arms and ammunition lying at
                    Aden, and any attempt to forward it to the authorities in Abyssinia, and to
                    report that 34 cases of cartridges and 8 cases containing guns, were permitted
                    by the Aden authorities to be shipped to Abyssinia.</p>
                <p>They left for Djibouti, November 3, 1922, at 6 P.M. on the British cruiser <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Crocus</hi>, accompanied by Paulos Manamano, the
                    Abyssinian representative, who in 1921 visited the United States and made the
                    purchase. The <hi rendition="#italic">Crocus</hi> likewise carried His Highness
                    Ras Tafari and his party, who had just made a short but formal visit to
                    Aden.</p>
                <p>Whether or not the arms and ammunition will be permitted to reach Abyssinia from
                    Djibouti without trouble, it is impossible to say. The representative, Paulos
                    Manamano, seemed to be quite confident in regard to this point, however. He
                    indicated that it was his belief, that the French and Italians had concurred in
                    the arrangement and that from now on, no further trouble would be
                    experienced.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Raymond Davis</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
